    "text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\) and \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 419^\\circ\\), we need to use the periodic and symmetric properties of the sine function. Specifically, we use the identity \\(\\sin x = \\sin (180^\\circ - x)\\) and the periodicity \\(\\sin x = \\sin (x + 360^\\circ k)\\) for any integer \\( k \\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\( 419^\\circ \\) to an angle between \\( 0^\\circ \\) and \\( 360^\\circ \\):\n\\[\n419^\\circ = 420^\\circ - 1^\\circ = 360^\\circ + 60^\\circ - 1^\\circ = 60^\\circ - 1^\\circ.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sin (60^\\circ - 1^\\circ) = \\sin (60^\\circ - 1^\\circ)\\), we can use the identity \\(\\sin (180^\\circ - x) = \\sin x\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 419^\\circ = \\sin (60^\\circ - 1^\\circ) = \\sin (180^\\circ - (60^\\circ - 1^\\circ)) = \\sin (180^\\circ - 60^\\circ + 1^\\circ) = \\sin (121^\\circ).\n\\]\nHowever, we need to find an equivalent angle between \\( -90^\\circ \\) and \\( 90^\\circ \\). We use the identity \\(\\sin x = \\sin (180^\\circ - x)\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 121^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin (-59^\\circ).\n\\]\nSince \\(-59^\\circ\\) is already within the range \\([-90^\\circ, 90^\\circ]\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin 419^\\circ = \\sin (-59^\\circ).\n\\]\nThus, the integer \\( n \\) is \\(-59\\). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-59}.\n\\]",
